{"step_index":170,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-23T12:26:39Z","content":"Created At: 2026-08-23T20:26:39+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-08-23T20:26:45+08:00\nThe search for \"best features for a stock screener web app python\" returned the following summary:\nBuilding a robust stock screener web app in Python requires a balance between data accessibility, user-friendly filtering, and responsive visualization. Based on current industry standards and popular development practices, here are the best features to include:\n\n### 1. Core Screening Features\n*   **Multi-Factor Filtering:** Allow users to filter by fundamental metrics (Market Cap, P/E Ratio, Dividend Yield, Revenue, Debt-to-Equity) and technical indicators (RSI, MACD, Moving Averages, Bollinger Bands, Volume, and VWAP).\n*   **Customizable Thresholds:** Instead of hard-coded values, use range sliders or input fields to let users set their own criteria (e.g., \"P/E between 10 and 20\").\n*   **Sector and Industry Categorization:** Enable filtering by sector or industry to allow users to compare companies within the same business vertical.\n\n### 2. Data & Performance\n*   **Reliable Data Sources:** Use reputable APIs like **yfinance** (for free, accessible data), **Alpha Vantage**, or **Financial Modeling Prep (FMP)** for more institutional-grade, structured data.\n*   **Efficient Caching:** Implement caching (e.g., `st.cache_data` in Streamlit) to minimize repeated API calls, reduce latency, and improve the user experience.\n*   **Real-Time Data Support:** If your project scales, consider using webhooks or professional APIs that support real-time market data to ensure users are not acting on stale information.\n\n### 3. User Experience (UX) & Visualization\n*   **Interactive Dashboards:** Use libraries like **Plotly** or **Dash** to create dynamic, interactive charts that update instantly when filters are applied.\n*   **Flexible Data Views:** Offer both list (table) and grid views for results, with clickable column headers for sorting (e.g., sorting by highest Market Cap).\n*   **Export Functionality:** Provide a \"Download to CSV/Excel\" feature so users can save their filtered results for further offline analysis.\n*   **Responsive UI:** Build with modern web frameworks like **Streamlit** (for quick, data-heavy dashboards) or **Dash/Flask** (for more complex, customized web apps).\n\n### 4. Advanced & \"Power User\" Features\n*   **Saved Scans/Alerts:** Allow users to save their filter configurations and receive notifications (email or dashboard alerts) when a new stock meets their criteria.\n*   **Multi-Timeframe Analysis:** Enable users to look at different time horizons simultaneously to confirm momentum across short-term and long-term trends.\n*   **Predictive/AI Insights:** Integrate basic machine learning models (e.g., using `scikit-learn`) to provide predictive scores or trend forecasts based on historical data.\n*   **Watchlists:** A feature for users to save specific tickers they are interested in, keeping them separate from the broader screening results.\n\n### Recommended Tech Stack\n*   **Backend/Data:** Python with `pandas` and `numpy` for data manipulation.\n*   **Framework:** \n    *   **Streamlit:** Best for rapid development and data science-heavy projects.\n    *   **Dash/Flask:** Better for production-grade applications that require extensive custom design and modular architecture.\n*   **Data Visualization:** `Plotly` is the gold standard for interactive financial charts in the Python ecosystem."}
